---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Reading from '../../layouts/Reading.astro';

// Prose carried over from the old about page, which Kevin wrote.
// Only the structure changed: the numbered section eyebrows are gone
// (the order already says it's a sequence) and the sidebar of facts
// moved to the end, since this design has one column.
import portraitMain from '../../assets/portrait/kevin-holler.jpg';
import gettingStarted from '../../assets/portrait/getting-started.jpg';
import linuxServers from '../../assets/portrait/linux-servers.jpg';
import shakeTeam from '../../assets/portrait/shake-team.jpg';
import shakeOffice from '../../assets/portrait/shake-office.jpg';
import khSetup from '../../assets/portrait/kh-setup.jpg';
import landscaping from '../../assets/portrait/landscaping.jpg';
import portrait2 from '../../assets/portrait/kevin-holler-2.jpg';
import ccc from '../../assets/portrait/ccc.jpg';

const pairSizes = '(max-width: 620px) 100vw, 344px';
const fullSizes = '(max-width: 44rem) 100vw, 704px';
---
<Reading
  title="About — Kevin Holler"
  description="Kevin Holler — a nonlinear career across engineering, sales, product, and data."
  here="about"
>
  <h1>A nonlinear career, chosen on purpose.</h1>

  <p>
    German-Irish, based in Würzburg. Spent the last 12 years moving between
    engineering, sales, product, and data — sometimes by plan, sometimes because
    the company was on fire and someone had to do it. These days I lead applied AI
    work at LaunchDarkly and build suky.ai on the side.
  </p>

  <figure class="figure">
    <Image src={portraitMain} alt="Kevin Holler." widths={[560, 704, 1408]} sizes={fullSizes} loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" />
  </figure>

  <p>
    I write code, talk to customers, and try to be useful — usually in that order.
    The thread connecting every job I've held is a stubborn belief that the most
    interesting problems live in the seams between teams.
  </p>

  <h2>How I got here</h2>
  <p>
    Born in Germany, raised in Ireland. My tech career began at 14 when I found
    Eric Raymond's "How to Become a Hacker" — that was the unlock. Python, Linux,
    building computers, freelancing as a product engineer while studying Creative
    Computing in Dublin.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pivotal moment was joining <strong>Contrast</strong>, a boutique software
    shop run by the now co-founders of <strong>Intercom</strong>. That's where I
    first saw startup culture up close — the iteration, the conviction, the comfort
    with chaos. It set the trajectory for everything that came after.
  </p>

  <div class="figure pair">
    <figure>
      <Image src={gettingStarted} alt="Kevin at 14, at his first computer setup, 2004." widths={[344, 688]} sizes={pairSizes} loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>2004 · the first setup</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <Image src={linuxServers} alt="Home Linux server setup, mid-2000s." widths={[344, 688]} sizes={pairSizes} loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Building Linux boxes</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <h2>The unconventional path</h2>
  <p>
    At <strong>Engine Yard</strong>, I made the unusual choice to join sales
    engineering instead of software development. Then to <strong>New Relic</strong>
    as the first SE on their EMEA founding team. That experience was rocket fuel
    for my first startup — <strong>Shake</strong> — a three-year journey that
    started with €50K from friends and family and grew to $250K from Silicon Valley
    investors.
  </p>
  <p>
    Shake didn't make it. I took on €30K of personal debt to wind it down properly
    and not leave anyone holding the bag. The whole experience taught me more about
    myself, people, and business than any other point in my life.
  </p>

  <div class="figure pair">
    <figure>
      <Image src={shakeTeam} alt="The Shake founding team." widths={[344, 688]} sizes={pairSizes} loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The Shake team · Dublin</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <Image src={shakeOffice} alt="The empty Shake office on the last day." widths={[344, 688]} sizes={pairSizes} loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Last day · Shake office</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <h2>The middle years</h2>
  <p>
    Post-Shake I moved into product management at <strong>ChangeX</strong>, then to
    Munich as a Data Architect, building analytics systems for global enterprises
    (including Pampers' loyalty app). My third venture, <strong>Trackplan</strong>,
    came out of my own frustration with behavioral tracking. We had strong traction
    and commitments for $750K of a planned $2M round — and lost the lead investor
    in due diligence at the last hour.
  </p>
  <p>
    The timing coincided with the birth of my son. What initially felt like a
    setback turned out to be a profound recalibration.
  </p>

  <h2>Applied AI, suky.ai, and what's next</h2>
  <p>
    Today I'm <strong>Staff Solutions Engineer</strong> at
    <strong>LaunchDarkly</strong>, where I lead our applied-AI motion across DACH —
    helping enterprise teams ship LLM features behind feature flags, with the
    experimentation and rollout discipline their other features get.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the side, I'm building <a href="/new/projects/">suky.ai</a> — a guardrail
    layer for behavioral event tracking. It's the tool I wish I'd had at every
    previous job that touched data.
  </p>

  <figure class="figure">
    <Image src={khSetup} alt="Workspace today." widths={[560, 704, 1408]} sizes={fullSizes} loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>The desk, today</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The slow stuff</h2>
  <p>
    Sourdough, garden, landscaping. I take on a manual project every year that has
    nothing to do with screens. Last year it was a dry-stone wall. This year, a
    small orchard. The kitchen experiments have escalated to the point where I keep
    a tasting journal.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other constant is community. <strong>Chaos Communication Camp</strong>
    every four years, a rotating dinner club, and the local Stammtisch — the people
    I've collected along the way are the part of the career I'm proudest of.
  </p>

  <div class="figure pair">
    <figure>
      <Image src={landscaping} alt="Garden landscaping project — playhouse and bench." widths={[344, 688]} sizes={pairSizes} loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Garden project · Würzburg</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <Image src={portrait2} alt="Cooking for friends at home." widths={[344, 688]} sizes={pairSizes} loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Sunday lunch · home</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <figure class="figure">
    <Image src={ccc} alt="Chaos Communication Camp installation at dusk." widths={[560, 704]} sizes={fullSizes} loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>CCC · the tribe</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    If any of this sounds like a problem you have — or a problem I've had —
    <a href="mailto:hello@kevinholler.com">say hello</a>.
  </p>

  <dl class="facts">
    <dt>Role</dt>
    <dd>Staff Solutions Engineer, LaunchDarkly — applied AI lead, DACH</dd>

    <dt>Building</dt>
    <dd>suky.ai — behavioral event contracts</dd>

    <dt>Based</dt>
    <dd>Würzburg, Germany</dd>

    <dt>Languages</dt>
    <dd>German &amp; English (native)</dd>

    <dt>Previously</dt>
    <dd>Trackplan · ChangeX · Pampers · New Relic · Engine Yard · Contrast · Shake</dd>

    <dt>Stack</dt>
    <dd>TypeScript · React · Next.js · Python · dbt · SQL · AWS · Postgres</dd>

    <dt>Education</dt>
    <dd>B.Sc. Creative Computing — IADT, Dublin</dd>
  </dl>
</Reading>
